      "answer": "Preparation is triggered by a planned outing, typically visible on a shared family calendar. The process starts by checking the outing time and venue information. Checking and packing usually takes about fifteen minutes, excluding travel. Spontaneous same-day decisions are not specified in the workflow."
    },
    {
      "question_id": "Q2",
      "question": "Who are the people you coordinate with before the outing, and how do you currently communicate with them to confirm plans or share information?",
      "answer": "Coordination involves the partner, who confirms who will accompany the child, and any accompanying caregivers, who are notified of plan changes. A venue contact is also relevant if the venue plan needs checking. The specific communication tools used are not specified beyond the shared family calendar."
    },
    {
      "question_id": "Q3",
      "question": "Can you walk me through the specific items or categories of items you gather before leaving, and does this list change depending on the type of outing or destination?",
      "answer": "The mother prepares an outing checklist based on the confirmed plan, then checks reusable items against a household inventory list before packing. The checklist is created from the confirmed plan details, implying it can vary by outing. Specific item categories are not listed in the workflow."
    },
    {
      "question_id": "Q4",
      "question": "What do you do if something goes wrong during preparation — for instance, if a key item is missing or a participant can no longer come?",
      "answer": "Two main exceptions are handled: if a required item is missing, the mother resolves it by obtaining a substitute or postponing the outing; if an accompanying caregiver becomes unavailable, the partner notifies other caregivers and the plan is rearranged. Venue plan changes are also a recognised exception."
    },
    {
      "question_id": "Q5",
      "question": "When you put things away after returning, what specific steps are involved — for example, cleaning, restocking, or storing items in particular places — and how do you know when everything is properly done?",
      "answer": "After returning, the mother records which items were used and restocks the bag. Restocking reminders can be generated from recorded usage. Completion is confirmed when the bag is restocked and the item list is updated. Specific cleaning or storage location details are not specified in the workflow."
    },
    {
      "question_id": "Q6",
      "question": "Are there any apps, tools, or systems you already use to support any part of this process, such as a shared calendar, a packing checklist app, or a messaging platform?",
      "answer": "The workflow references a shared family calendar, an outing checklist, a venue information page, and a household inventory list. The family keeps an item list, but it does not detect physical stock automatically. Specific app names or messaging platforms are not specified."
    }
  ]
